Despite my repeated requests for clarification and escalation, Avant representatives consistently provided scripted recitations of declined transactions already known to me, rather than substantive information regarding the cause of the authorization failures or whether my account was subject to any authorization-level restrictions, enhanced monitoring, or internal risk controls. Representatives repeatedly stated the card should work, which proved inaccurate, while failing to disclose material information necessary for me to understand or access my available credit. 

To the extent Avant possesses internal information affecting card usability, including authorization-layer controls, risk flags, or monitoring that restricts transactions despite available credit being displayed, the failure to disclose such information raises potential concerns under the Consumer Financial Protection Act, 12 U.S.C. 5536, which prohibits unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts or practices, including omissions of material information while making representations that an account should function normally. 

Additionally, where internal data or account-level determinations affect a consumers access to credit, the failure to ensure accuracy, transparency, and meaningful disclosure may implicate obligations under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 U.S.C. 1681, including the requirement that information affecting a consumers credit access be handled accurately and disclosed appropriately upon inquiry. 

By repeatedly reciting transaction history without disclosing the true cause of the authorization failures or escalating to a team capable of resolving or explaining the issue, Avant deprived me of meaningful information necessary to understand and use my credit account. This omission materially affected my ability to access credit that was represented as available and in good standing.",,"Avant Holding Company, Inc.",MN,55408,,Web,2025-12-24,Closed with explanation,Yes
