18278765,2025-12-25,Credit card,General-purpose credit card or charge card,Problem with a purchase shown on your statement,Card was charged for something you did not purchase with the card,"I am filing a complaint regarding Chase Banks handling of my credit card fraud claim ( card ending in XXXX ) involving unauthorized purchases made through a digital marketplace app called XXXX. 

The transactions were not authorized by me, and I did not receive or benefit from any of the goods purchased. The items were likely received by my ex-spouse, who at the time had physical access to my phone but did not have authorization to make purchases on my account. 

Chase denied my fraud claim solely on the basis that the transactions originated from my device, and I was explicitly told by Chase that because the purchases were made by my ex-spouse, this was a personal matter and not the banks responsibility ( XXXX, a fraud representative for Chase ). 

Chases denial is inconsistent with federal consumer protection law. 

Under the Fair Credit Billing Act ( 15 U.S.C. 1666 ), a transaction is considered unauthorized if it is made without the cardholders actual authority, regardless of whether it was made using the cardholders device or credentials. 

Physical access to a device does not constitute authorization. A third party making purchases without permission even a spouse or former spouse is unauthorized use under federal law. 

Additionally : I did not receive the merchandise I did not benefit from the transactions I did not authorize the purchases I reported the activity promptly The individual responsible has since been removed from all access Chases position that this is a personal matter improperly shifts liability to the consumer and ignores the statutory definition of unauthorized use. 


Important notes : These were credit card transactions ( not debit ) The charges were made through a third-party digital marketplace ( XXXX ) I did not receive any of the purchased items Chase denied the claim solely because the transactions came from my device I was explicitly told this was a personal matter and not Chases responsibility I am requesting CFPB assistance to ensure Chase Bank complies with federal consumer protection laws governing unauthorized credit card transactions.",,JPMORGAN CHASE & CO.,SC,29036,Servicemember,Web,2025-12-25,Closed with explanation,Yes
